The arrivals are processed from earliest to latest. A min heap stores the departure time of every train currently using a platform. Before handling a new arrival, all trains that have already departed are removed from the heap.

The heap size is the number of platforms in use at that moment. This also handles trains with the same arrival or departure time using the usual inclusive comparison.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int findPlatform(int[] Arrival, int[] Departure) {
        int n = Arrival.length;
        if (n == 0) return 0;

        Integer[] order = new Integer[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            order[i] = i;
        }

        Arrays.sort(order, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(Arrival[a], Arrival[b]));

        PriorityQueue<Integer> activeDepartures = new PriorityQueue<>();
        int answer = 0;

        for (int index : order) {
            while (!activeDepartures.isEmpty()
                    && activeDepartures.peek() < Arrival[index]) {
                activeDepartures.poll();
            }

            activeDepartures.offer(Departure[index]);
            answer = Math.max(answer, activeDepartures.size());
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

An empty schedule returns zero. Since the heap keeps one departure per active train, its extra space can reach `O(n)`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

For every train, count how many other trains are present when it arrives. The maximum of these counts is the number of platforms needed. This checks all pairs directly, so it is slower than sorting with two pointers, but the condition is easy to verify.

A train is active at time `t` when its arrival is at or before `t` and its departure is at or after `t`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public:
    int findPlatform(vector<int>& Arrival, vector<int>& Departure){
        int n = Arrival.size();
        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int active = 0;

            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (Arrival[j] <= Arrival[i] && Arrival[i] <= Departure[j]) {
                    active++;
                }
            }

            answer = max(answer, active);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For a single train, the inner loop counts one active train, so the result is one. If the arrays are empty, the loops do not run and zero is returned.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The arrays can be sorted separately and checked at each arrival time. By that time, the number of trains that have arrived is the count of arrivals less than or equal to the current time. The number that has left is the count of departures strictly before it.

Subtracting those two values gives the platforms still occupied. Two binary searches are used for each arrival.

Dry run with arrivals `[900, 940]` and departures `[910, 1200]`:

- At 900, arrivals so far are 1 and departures before 900 are 0, so active is 1.
- At 940, arrivals so far are 2 and departures before 940 are 1, so active is also 1.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int findPlatform(int[] Arrival, int[] Departure) {
        int n = Arrival.length;
        if (n == 0) return 0;

        int[] arrivals = Arrival.clone();
        int[] departures = Departure.clone();
        Arrays.sort(arrivals);
        Arrays.sort(departures);

        int answer = 0;

        for (int time : arrivals) {
            int arrived = upperBound(arrivals, time);
            int departed = lowerBound(departures, time);
            answer = Math.max(answer, arrived - departed);
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private int lowerBound(int[] values, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = values.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (values[middle] < target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }

    private int upperBound(int[] values, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = values.length;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (values[middle] <= target) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
}
```

The original arrays are not changed because this code sorts copies. Equal arrival and departure times keep the train active, since departures are counted only when they are strictly earlier.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting and binary searches.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied arrays.

Sorting arrivals and departures makes the platform count easy to track. If the next train arrives before the earliest departure, another platform is needed. Otherwise, one platform becomes free and can be reused.

For example, with arrivals `[900, 940, 950]` and departures `[910, 1200, 1120]`, the first train uses one platform. The 940 train arrives before 910 is processed as a free platform, so the count becomes two.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution{
    public:
    int findPlatform(vector<int>& Arrival, vector<int>& Departure){
        int n = Arrival.size();
        if (n == 0) return 0;

        sort(Arrival.begin(), Arrival.end());
        sort(Departure.begin(), Departure.end());

        int arrivalIndex = 0;
        int departureIndex = 0;
        int currentPlatforms = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        while (arrivalIndex < n) {
            if (Arrival[arrivalIndex] <= Departure[departureIndex]) {
                currentPlatforms++;
                answer = max(answer, currentPlatforms);
                arrivalIndex++;
            } else {
                currentPlatforms--;
                departureIndex++;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the sorting space. The input arrays are changed by sorting them.

## Intuition
Let's formulate a way to replicate trains waiting on a platform.

## Approach
Use a minheap to track trains waiting.
Sort trains chronologically by arrival times.
If a train looks a all the other trains and sees that it needs a platform, create one for it by adding it to the minHeap.

Now if a train sees that when it arrives, the other previous trains can leave before it comes, then simulate the same by popping those trains from the MinHeap.

Now the max no. of platforms, is the max size of minHeap at any point of time.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{
    public:
    int findPlatform(vector<int>& Arrival, vector<int>& Departure){
        // find the maximum no. of intervals overlapping
        vector<vector<int>> times;
        for(int i=0; i<Arrival.size(); i++)
            times.push_back({Arrival[i], Departure[i]});

        sort(times.begin(), times.end()); // sort by arrival times

        int max_count = 1; // we need atleast one platform
        int s, e, i=1;

        priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minHeap; // storing the departure times of trains already idling on the platform
        minHeap.push(times.front().back());
        while(i < times.size()){
            s = times[i].front();
            e = times[i].back();

            while(!minHeap.empty() && s > minHeap.top()){
                // the train can safely replace the train on a platform
                // so remove all waiting trains
                minHeap.pop();
            }

            minHeap.push(e);
            max_count = max(max_count, (int)minHeap.size());
            i++;
        }
        return max_count;
    }
};
```

